[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Type Guards and Narrowing in TypeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript's type system is designed to be expressive yet static. Often, you will work with variables that can hold multiple types, known as union types. To safely interact with these variables, TypeScript uses a process called type narrowing. Type guards are expressions that perform runtime checks to guarantee the type in a specific scope."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Primitive Type Narrowing with typeof"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The simplest form of a type guard is the built-in JavaScript 'typeof' operator. TypeScript understands this operator and uses it to narrow down primitive types such as string, number, boolean, and symbol."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"function formatInput(input: string | number) {
  if (typeof input === 'string') {
    return input.trim().toUpperCase();
  }
  return input.toFixed(2);
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Class Instance Narrowing with instanceof"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"When dealing with object-oriented code, the 'instanceof' operator acts as a type guard for checking whether an object is an instance of a specific class or constructor function."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"class AdminUser {
  constructor(public name: string) {}
  adminPrivileges() { console.log('Admin access granted'); }
}

class GuestUser {
  constructor(public name: string) {}
}

function handleUser(user: AdminUser | GuestUser) {
  if (user instanceof AdminUser) {
    user.adminPrivileges();
  } else {
    console.log('Welcome guest: ' + user.name);
  }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Custom Type Guards Using Type Predicates"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"For complex or custom data structures (like interfaces or raw JSON objects), built-in guards are not enough. You can define a custom type guard using a type predicate function with the return type syntax 'parameterName is Type'."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"interface Fish {
  swim(): void;
}

interface Bird {
  fly(): void;
}

function isFish(pet: Fish | Bird): pet is Fish {
  return (pet as Fish).swim !== undefined;
}

function move(pet: Fish | Bird) {
  if (isFish(pet)) {
    pet.swim();
  } else {
    pet.fly();
  }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Narrowing"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always prefer native operators like 'typeof' and 'instanceof' before writing custom guards."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure your custom type predicates return accurate boolean evaluations to avoid runtime errors."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Utilize discriminated unions with a common literal property for clean, scalable state handling."}]
